# .trivyignore — waivers for Trivy findings you have reviewed and accepted.
#
# Add one CVE ID per line. Comments above the ID are part of the file's git
# history and become your audit trail for why a finding is not being fixed.
#
# Example:
#
#   # CVE-2025-12345 — libcurl in upstream base. Not reachable from our
#   # deployment (container runs behind mTLS ingress, no outbound HTTP).
#   # Revisit 2026-08 when upstream bumps the base image.
#   CVE-2025-12345
#
# Leave this file empty (except for comments) until you actually need a waiver.
# A waiver without a reason is just a mute button, not evidence.

# CVE-2026-34986 — go-jose JWE decryption panic (HIGH, DoS only).
# Fix lands in go-jose v4.1.4; Gokapi v2.2.4 ships an older version
# and is expected to bump in v2.2.5.
#
# Exploit prerequisite: the JWE decryption path must be active at
# runtime, which only happens when OIDC authentication is configured
# in Gokapi. THEKROLL runs Gokapi internal-only with no OIDC/SSO
# integration, so the vulnerable code path is unreachable.
#
# Impact class: denial of service via panic — not data exposure,
# not code execution. Worst case is an attacker who can already
# trigger an OIDC flow (which they can't here) crashes the service.
#
# Revisit when Gokapi v2.2.5 ships the go-jose bump,
# or by 2026-07 — whichever comes first.
CVE-2026-34986

# ============================================================================
# golang.org/x/crypto/ssh CVE family (9 CVEs, all HIGH) — module version-match
# FALSE POSITIVE. The vulnerable SSH code is not compiled into the Gokapi
# binary at all.
# ============================================================================
# CVE-2026-39828, -39829, -39830, -39831, -39832, -39835, -42508, -46595,
# -46597 are all flagged against golang.org/x/crypto v0.48.0 (fix v0.52.0).
# Every one lives in an SSH subpackage: x/crypto/ssh, x/crypto/ssh/agent, or
# x/crypto/ssh/knownhosts (command execution, DoS, source-address / revocation
# / user-presence bypasses on an SSH server or agent).
#
# Why this is a false positive rather than a reachability judgement:
#   Trivy reads the MODULE version from the Go binary's build metadata
#   (`golang.org/x/crypto v0.48.0`) and reports every CVE registered against
#   that module — it does NOT record which PACKAGES were actually linked.
#   Go only compiles imported packages into the binary. Gokapi v2.2.4 imports
#   exactly one x/crypto subpackage — `golang.org/x/crypto/scrypt` (verified:
#   `grep -rhoE '"golang.org/x/crypto/[a-z0-9/]+"'` over Forceu/Gokapi@v2.2.4
#   returns only scrypt). The x/crypto module is pulled in *for scrypt*; the
#   `ssh` / `ssh/agent` / `ssh/knownhosts` trees are never imported.
#
#   Nor does any dependency drag them in: Gokapi's require graph has no SSH,
#   SFTP, git, docker, or k8s client — the classic x/crypto/ssh importers.
#   (aws-sdk-go, coreos/go-oidc, go-jose, oauth2, modernc/sqlite, gopsutil,
#   miniredis, … none import x/crypto/ssh.) The vulnerable functions are
#   therefore absent from the linked binary, not merely dormant.
#
# Not rebuild-closable and no rebuild is warranted: v2.2.4 is the latest
# upstream tag and go.mod pins x/crypto v0.48.0, so a rebuild yields the
# identical module set. Bumping x/crypto to v0.52.0 would mean patching
# go.mod (a source-code fork, against this repo's build-system-fork-only
# invariant) and would not change the security posture, since the ssh code
# is not linked either way.
#
# Revisit when Gokapi bumps golang.org/x/crypto to >= v0.52.0 (the finding
# clears on its own then and these lines can be removed), or by 2026-11 —
# whichever comes first. Reconfirm at that point that no SSH/SFTP-importing
# dependency has been added to Gokapi's graph.
CVE-2026-39828
CVE-2026-39829
CVE-2026-39830
CVE-2026-39831
CVE-2026-39832
CVE-2026-39835
CVE-2026-42508
CVE-2026-46595
CVE-2026-46597